lopedia/thumbs/badr-al-jamali.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Badr al-Jamali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Badr al-Jamali was an Armenian commander who became vizier of the Fatimid Caliphate in 1073 after being summoned to restore order during a severe crisis.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/badr-ibn-hasanwayh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/badr-ibn-hasanwayh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Badr ibn Hasanwayh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Badr ibn Hasanwayh was the most prominent ruler of the Kurdish Hasanwayhid dynasty in the central Zagros, governing from 979 to 1014 under nominal Buyid overlordship.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/badra-iraq"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/badra-iraq.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Badra, Iraq</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Badra is a town in Wasit Governorate in eastern Iraq, close to the Iranian border at the foot of the Zagros mountains.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/badrinath"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/badrinath.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Badrinath</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Badrinath is a Hindu pilgrimage town in Chamoli district, Uttarakhand, India, high in the Garhwal Himalaya on the Alaknanda river.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/baduy"><span class="enc-name">Baduy</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Baduy, or Kanekes, are a Sundanese community living in the hills of Lebak Regency in Banten, western Java, Indonesia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/bae-jung-son"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bae-jung-son.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Bae Jung-son</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Bae Jung-son was a Goryeo military officer who led the Sambyeolcho units in revolt in 1270, when the court returned to Kaegyong and accepted Mongol overlordship.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/baekje-historic-areas"><span class="enc-name">Baekje Historic Areas</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Baekje Historic Areas are a UNESCO World Heritage Site in South Korea, listed in 2015.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/baetan-mac-cairill"><span class="enc-name">Báetán mac Cairill</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Báetán mac Cairill was a king of the Dál Fiatach and over-king of the Ulaid in north-eastern Ireland who died in 581.</span></a></li></ul>
